    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The maximum effective diameter of the human birth canal (pelvic inlet) is approximately 11-12 cm in the anteroposterior dimension and 12-13 cm in the transverse dimension.",
      "evidence": "Standard obstetric measurements show the pelvic inlet is the widest part of the canal. The 'true conjugate' (effective AP) is ~11 cm, and the transverse diameter is ~12-13 cm. The outlet is narrower than the inlet.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The adult human skull is a rigid, fully ossified structure that cannot deform to reduce its dimensions without fracture.",
      "evidence": "Unlike fetal skulls which have unfused fontanelles and sutures allowing for molding (compression), adult cranial sutures are fused and the bones are fully ossified. Significant deformation requires fracture, which destroys the integrity of the skull.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The birth canal is a rigid bony structure that cannot expand to accommodate an object significantly larger than its normal dimensions without catastrophic fracture.",
      "evidence": "The pelvic ring is a stable bony structure. While soft tissues can stretch, the bony boundaries of the pelvis do not expand to accommodate a rigid object larger than the inlet without shattering the pelvis, which is not a physiological 'passing through'.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore, an adult human skull cannot physically pass through the birth canal intact.",
      "evidence": "Combining the size mismatch (Step 1 vs Step 2), the lack of deformability of the skull (Step 3), and the rigidity of the pelvis (Step 4), the object is too large and rigid to traverse the canal without destroying either the skull or the pelvis.",
